 So, we can use the sum identity for cosine twice, with $u = \theta$ and $v = 2 \theta$. Let’s start with \begin{align*}
\cos 3 \theta &= \cos (2 \theta + \theta) \\
&= \cos 2 \theta \cos \theta - \sin 2 \theta \sin \theta.
\end{align*}
We can use the double angle formulas to see that \begin{align*}
\cos 3 \theta &= \cos^2 \theta \cos \theta - \sin^2 \theta \cos \theta - 2 \sin^2 \theta \cos \theta \\
&= \cos^3 \theta - \sin^2 \theta \cos \theta - 2 \sin^2 \theta \cos \theta \\
&= \cos^3 \theta - 3 \sin^2 \theta \cos \theta.
\end{align*}
Using that $\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta = 1,$ we see that \begin{align*}
\cos 3 \theta &= \cos^3 \theta - 3 (1 - \cos^2 \theta) \cos \theta \\
&= \cos^3 \theta - 3 \cos \theta + 3 \cos^3 \theta \\
&= 4 \cos^3 \theta - 3 \cos \theta.
\end{align*}
Now, note that $\cos \theta = \frac{1}{4}.$ We can thus cube and multiply by $4$ to obtain: \begin{align*}
\cos 3 \theta &= 4 \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^3 - 3 \left( \frac{1}{4}\right) \\
&= 4 \left( \frac{1}{64} \right) - \frac{3}{4} \\
&= \frac{1}{16} - \frac{12}{16} \\
&= -\frac{11}{16}.
\end{align*}
Therefore, $\boxed{\cos 3 \theta = -\frac{11}{16}.}$
